LINE CODES ON THE MF8 FRAME -- structural properties, measured
  Intervals and disparity in BIT periods.  min interval sets the max
  fundamental; max interval sets what clock recovery must coast; the
  disparity swing is the DC wander a transformer has to pass.

  --- payload: random  (line ones density 0.513)
  code                       levels  min int  max int   mean DC  disparity
  Manchester (biphase-L)          2     0.50     1.00    +0.000        1.0
  differential Manchester         2     0.50     1.00    +0.000        1.0
  AMI                             3     1.00    15.00    +0.000        1.0
  unipolar NRZ                    2     1.00    17.00    +0.513    16419.5
  unipolar RZ                     2     0.50    15.50    +0.257     8209.5
  polar NRZ                       2     1.00    17.00    +0.026      854.0
  NRZI                            2     1.00    16.00    +0.006      257.0
  CMI                             2     0.50     1.50    +0.000        1.5
  Miller                          2     1.00     2.00    -0.002      109.0

  --- payload: const0  (line ones density 0.141)
  code                       levels  min int  max int   mean DC  disparity
  Manchester (biphase-L)          2     0.50     1.00    +0.000        1.0
  differential Manchester         2     0.50     1.00    +0.000        1.0
  AMI                             3     1.00    15.00    +0.000        1.0
  unipolar NRZ                    2     1.00    15.00    +0.141     4499.5
  unipolar RZ                     2     0.50    15.50    +0.070     2249.5
  polar NRZ                       2     1.00    15.00    -0.719    23001.0
  NRZI                            2     4.00    16.00    +0.000       16.0
  CMI                             2     0.50     1.50    +0.000        1.5
  Miller                          2     1.00     1.50    +0.000        2.0

  --- payload: const1  (line ones density 0.891)
  code                       levels  min int  max int   mean DC  disparity
  Manchester (biphase-L)          2     0.50     1.00    +0.000        1.0
  differential Manchester         2     0.50     1.00    +0.000        1.0
  AMI                             3     1.00     1.00    +0.000        1.0
  unipolar NRZ                    2     1.00    23.00    +0.891    28499.5
  unipolar RZ                     2     0.50     1.50    +0.445    14249.5
  polar NRZ                       2     1.00    23.00    +0.781    24999.5
  NRZI                            2     1.00     2.00    +0.000        2.0
  CMI                             2     0.50     1.50    +0.000        1.5
  Miller                          2     1.00     2.00    +0.000        2.0

  The disparity column decides the candidate set, because the pair is
  transformer coupled (mf8-spec.md 9.3) and a transformer cannot pass
  unbounded DC wander.  Bounded at order one bit period: Manchester,
  differential Manchester, AMI, CMI.  Miller and NRZI halve the
  bandwidth and would be the interesting candidates, and both fail
  here.  Everything else fails by orders of magnitude.

ZERO RUNS -- the frame bounds them, so AMI needs no zero substitution
  Worst run of zeros on the line, over all 64 hook states.

  const0          15
  const1           1
  silence          3
  speech           2
  random          12

  worst over everything: 15 bits.
  F is 0 one frame in eight, so a mark lands at worst every 2 frames =
  16 line bits whatever the payload does.  B8ZS substitutes at 8 zeros
  and HDB3 at 4, so neither is needed -- the framing bit does the job.


OPTION B -- analog tank (rectify, resonator, flywheel)
  Sampling phase trimmed once at nominal: phase 0, BER 0.0e+00.
  BER on the worst payload (constant 0), tank detuned:

       Q  amp left    0.00%    0.10%    0.25%    0.50%    1.00%    2.00%
      20      0.09 1.4e-01 1.4e-01 1.4e-01 1.4e-01 1.4e-01 1.4e-01
      50      0.39 1.6e-01 7.8e-02 7.8e-02 9.4e-02 1.2e-01 1.4e-01
     100      0.62      ok      ok      ok      ok 1.2e-01 1.4e-01
     200      0.79      ok      ok      ok 4.2e-05 1.2e-01 1.4e-01
     500      0.91      ok      ok      ok 9.3e-02 1.4e-01 1.4e-01

  Two effects pull opposite ways, and the second binds:
    coasting decay over 15 idle cycles = exp(-pi*15/Q)  -> wants Q high
    static phase shift when detuned    = atan(2*Q*df/f) -> wants Q low
  The measured edge sits at df/f ~ 1/(2Q).  A tank wants Q ~ 100 AND
  tuning inside ~0.25%: a ceramic resonator, not an LC.

WHY AMI NEEDS A TIME REFERENCE AND DIFFERENTIAL MANCHESTER DOES NOT
  It is the DYNAMIC RANGE of the timing measurement:

    diff Manchester  intervals are 0.5 or 1.0 cell  -> 2:1, +/-13% ok
    AMI              gaps run 1 to 16 bit periods   -> 16:1, needs
                     tol < 0.5/16 = 3.1%

OPTION A -- no clock at all: quantise the gap between marks

   RC tol  random BER   count |  const 0 BER    count
     0.0%     0.0e+00      -2 |      0.0e+00      -15
     0.5%     0.0e+00      -2 |      0.0e+00      -15
     1.0%     0.0e+00      -2 |      0.0e+00      -15
     2.0%     0.0e+00      -2 |      0.0e+00      -15
     3.0%     0.0e+00      -2 |      0.0e+00      -15
     5.0%     4.2e-01     -35 |      2.4e-01     -764
    10.0%     4.9e-01   -1068 |      2.5e-01    -3764

  Error-free to 2%, broken at 3% -- the 3.1% predicted.  A 1% resistor
  and a 2% C0G give +/-3%, so a clockless AMI receiver sits ON its
  failure point.  And it fails by SLIPPING: a mis-rounded gap emits the
  wrong number of zeros, handing back the one property (4) that makes
  AMI worth considering.  Not a real option.


OPTION C -- oversampling DPLL off the local 4.096 MHz crystal
  4.096 MHz divides every family rate exactly: 1024 kbps 4x, 512 kbps 8x, 256 kbps 16x, 128 kbps 32x, 64 kbps 64x

  The crystal is a SAMPLING reference; the DPLL recovers bit phase
  from the line, so the bit clock stays locked to the far transmitter.

   crystal offset   drift over clip   bits emitted - sent       BER
             0 ppm            0.0 bits                     0   0.0e+00
           100 ppm            4.8 bits                     0   0.0e+00
           200 ppm            9.6 bits                     0   0.0e+00
          1000 ppm           48.0 bits                     0   0.0e+00
          5000 ppm          240.0 bits                     0   0.0e+00

  The bit count is exact at every offset.  The DPLL absorbs drift as
  PHASE, not as bits, so mf8-spec.md 2's slip-free property survives:
  a local crystal used as a SAMPLING reference does not cause slips.
  What it costs is the receiver's clocklessness, and nothing else.

POLARITY -- is AMI blind to a tip/ring swap, as biphase is?

  A swap inverts everything arriving differentially, so the test is to
  invert the complete received waveform, impairments included.

  condition              code              identical  bits differing
  clean                  diff Manchester         YES               0
  clean                  AMI                     YES               0
  noise sd 0.4           diff Manchester         YES               0
  noise sd 0.4           AMI                     YES               0
  noise sd 0.6           diff Manchester         YES               0
  noise sd 0.6           AMI                     YES               0
  impulse 100/s          diff Manchester         YES               0
  impulse 100/s          AMI                     YES               0
  attenuated 0.25x       diff Manchester         YES               0
  attenuated 0.25x       AMI                     YES               0

  All conditions bit-identical: True.

  Differential Manchester decides same-vs-different half cells, and
  inversion flips both.  AMI decides |x| > thresh*peak, so the sign
  never enters -- and a full-wave front end is MANDATORY for AMI
  anyway, because marks arrive as both + and - pulses.  Polarity
  neutrality falls out of the receiver you must build regardless.
  The alternation rule is symmetric too, so even bipolar-violation
  error detection survives a swap.

LINE IMPAIRMENTS, SWEPT INDEPENDENTLY -- random data, both codes
  16000 frames x 4 seeds per cell.  Each impairment applied
  on its own.  "raw BER" is the PHY alone; a slip re-phases the stream,
  so raw BER reads high once slips start -- read LOF and down % then.

  GAUSSIAN FLOOR, sd referred to the transmitted peak

