  ATTENUATION, sd 0.10 floor and 0.05 hysteresis held fixed

       atten code                raw BER    LOF   down %
           1 diff Manchester     0.0e+00      0    0.25%
           1 AMI                 0.0e+00      0    0.25%
         0.5 diff Manchester     0.0e+00      0    0.25%
         0.5 AMI                 0.0e+00      0    0.25%
        0.25 diff Manchester     0.0e+00      0    0.25%
        0.25 AMI                 5.1e-05      0    0.25%
        0.12 diff Manchester     5.0e-01    434   53.48%
        0.12 AMI                 4.2e-01     42   72.89%

  Differential Manchester holds a Gaussian floor to sd 0.6 where AMI is
  degraded from 0.4, and the attenuation cliff lands where the 0.05
  hysteresis passes 20% of the received amplitude (mf8-spec.md 9.3).
  Under IMPULSE the ranking reverses -- see 6 for the mechanism.

IMPULSE SUSCEPTIBILITY

  The rings are 15-80 kHz against a 512 kHz symbol rate, so each acts
  as a slowly varying OFFSET on the timescale of a bit.  With the signal
  at +/-1 and hysteresis h, an offset of magnitude A sticks the
  comparator when A > 1-h, and every transition while that holds is
  lost.  Each event is a DAMPED SINUSOID with a randomly signed
  amplitude, so it swings BOTH polarities many times.

  model check, 0.5 s at 200/s: min -3.165  max +2.859  mean -1.27e-04
  below -0.1: 0.0193   above +0.1: 0.0191   -- symmetric

  Bits gained or lost vs bits sent, 200 events/s, 4 seeds.  A count
  change is a SLIP: the frame re-phases and must reacquire.

   amplitude                    diff Manchester   AMI: count  AMI: raw BER
        0.50           [-640, -813, -823, -516] [-6, -4, -3, -1]       4.2e-01

  (the amplitude sweep proper)

   amplitude     diff Manchester: bits lost   AMI: bits lost
        0.50                     [640, 813]           [3, 4]
        0.80                     [640, 813]           [3, 4]
        0.95                     [640, 813]           [3, 4]
        1.10                     [640, 813]           [3, 4]
        1.50                     [640, 813]           [3, 4]
        3.00                     [640, 813]           [3, 4]

  MITIGATIONS

  hysteresis  threshold 1-h   bits lost, 200/s amp 0.9
        0.02           0.98                 [628, 785]
        0.05           0.95                 [640, 813]
        0.10           0.90                 [687, 874]
        0.20           0.80                [817, 1032]
  -> hysteresis moves the threshold the WRONG way, so the
     impulse-optimal comparator is a tight one.

   dead time     bits lost, 200/s
        0.05           [625, 790]
        0.15           [628, 796]
        0.25           [640, 813]
        0.35           [749, 955]
        0.45         [1087, 1385]
  -> dead time rejects edges that arrive too EARLY; a stuck comparator
     REMOVES edges, so there is nothing for it to reject.

   HP corner   diff Manchester LOF   AMI LOF   (100 imp/s)
        none                    15         3
      60 kHz                     5         0
     120 kHz                     4         0
     200 kHz                     4         0
  -> AC coupling helps differential Manchester; the lowest signal
     fundamental is 256 kHz so a 120 kHz corner is free.  AMI's
     spectrum reaches lower, so the same filter costs it signal.

THE FAMILY.  F + S + N channels must be a power of two, so N = 2, 6, 14

   frame   N  per-ch    line      baud  capacity  reach  superframe
      4b   2     16k     64k    0.128M       32k  2.00x        4 fr
      4b   2     32k    128k    0.256M       64k  1.41x        4 fr
      4b   2     64k    256k    0.512M      128k  1.00x        4 fr

      8b   6     16k    128k    0.256M       96k  1.41x        8 fr
      8b   6     32k    256k    0.512M      192k  1.00x        8 fr
      8b   6     64k    512k    1.024M      384k  0.71x        8 fr

     16b  14     16k    256k    0.512M      224k  1.00x       16 fr
     16b  14     32k    512k    1.024M      448k  0.71x       16 fr
     16b  14     64k   1024k    2.048M      896k  0.50x       16 fr

  Reach is the sqrt(f) law scaled to a 256 kbps reference; the ratios
  are sound and the kilometres are not measured.

  Read it by COLUMN: the line rate sets reach, the frame length decides
  how that line is divided.  64 kbps channels are available at ANY
  reach -- two of them at 1.00x, six at 0.71x.

WHAT THE PER-CHANNEL RATE COSTS THE AUDIO

   per-channel    segSNR  vs 64 kbps
          16k  11.17 dB   -14.01 dB
          32k  18.06 dB    -7.12 dB
          64k  25.18 dB    +0.00 dB

  32 kbps is the quality knee, 16 kbps is a real sacrifice, 64 kbps
  is comfortable -- and only 64 kbps is one-for-one with a DS0
  (mf8-spec.md 6): the 8-frame superframe IS the octet counter, and at
  a 64 kHz frame rate the superframe rate is 8 kHz exactly.

REACH IS NOT FREE EITHER -- the same span at three line rates
  Attenuation goes as sqrt(f), referenced to 12 dB at 256 kbps.

     line    loss  amplitude  hysteresis % of rx
     128k    8.5dB      0.376                 13%
     256k   12.0dB      0.251                 20%
     512k   17.0dB      0.142                 35%

  mf8-spec.md 9.3 specifies hysteresis at or under 10% of received, and
  20% is the single-span maximum, so the 512 kbps column is already
  outside it on this span.  That is the real case for a lower rate.

